---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { updated } from "../lib/data";
import { REPO, AMBIENT_REPO, CONTACT, ambient } from "../lib/site";
---
<Base title="Methodology" description="How vlm.fyi measures request limits: synthetic probe requests that isolate one limit at a time.">
  <div class="prose page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Methodology</span>
    <h1>Why we built this?</h1>
    <br />
    <p>
      Vlm.fyi is made by the team behind <a href={ambient("methodology")}>Ambient</a>, the
      <a href={AMBIENT_REPO}>open-source</a> video engine for AI agents. Ambient sends videos to many providers, and
      none of them documents these limits fully. So we measured them, and Ambient now sizes each request from this data.
      We publish it because everyone building with vision models runs into the same walls.
    <br />
    <br />
      At <a href={ambient("methodology")}>Ambient</a>, we have burned our fingers several times because our video requests were randomly routed to a provider
      that silently downsampled our videos, causing our agent's performance to degrade. Not only that, but even if we
      pinned a provider for a specific model, the provider would change its limits over time, causing our pipeline to
      break.
      <br />
      <br />
      To address this, we built an internal service to periodically probe and monitor limits. During the process, we
      realized that this could be useful to the community at large, so we decided to open-source it. We hope this helps
      anyone building video pipelines with LLMs create a robust provider-selection process and avoid surprises. This is 
      not going to be a static dataset, but rather a live dataset that is updated periodically. The updates will be published
      in our website and API. We are excited to share it with the community and looking forward to your feedback.
      <br />
    </p>
    <br />
    <br />
    <h1>How we measure</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We run several probe requests to the provider's OpenAI-compatible chat completions API to measure request limits.
    </p>

    <h2>Testing the limits</h2>
    <p>
      A real video request can hit several limits at once: the image count, the request size, the decoded pixels, the
      context window. To tell them apart, each probe sends a minimal request ("Reply with OK", 16 output tokens) carrying
      synthetic media designed to stress only one of them:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Images per request:</strong> tiny 64×64 solid images, so bytes and pixels stay negligible and only the count grows.</li>
      <li><strong>768×432 frames:</strong> real-size frames. 768 px on the long edge is a common working size for video pipelines (providers tile or rescale images around it), and 432 px makes it 16:9, the shape of most video. This reports whichever limit a realistic workload hits first. Limits on pixels and tokens scale with frame size: 1280×720 frames would fit about 2.8× fewer; image-count limits don't change.</li>
      <li><strong>Inline request body:</strong> tiny images padded with JPEG comment segments, which decoders ignore. The body grows; the pixels don't.</li>
      <li><strong>Images by URL:</strong> the same padded images, hosted and sent as https links, so the provider downloads them itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Video file size:</strong> a 5-second clip padded with an MP4 <code>free</code> box, which players ignore. The file grows; the content doesn't. Providers that refuse video URLs get the same clip inline (base64), up to 96 MB of video in a 128 MB request body.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For each limit we double the size until a request fails, then bisect between the last pass and the first failure.
      We also check which request fields are accepted, and whether tool results for parallel tool calls must directly follow
      the assistant message.
    </p>

    <h2 id="video-density">Video density: how much of a video the model sees</h2>
    <p>
      Sending a video doesn't tell you how much of it reaches the model. Providers sample and resize frames themselves,
      and for the same model id the amount differs by more than 100×. We measure it from the provider's own billing,
      never from the model's reply.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Video tokens per minute.</strong> We send one real reference clip (5 minutes 8 seconds, 1080p) as a <code>video_url</code>, twice, and subtract the prompt tokens billed for the same prompt without it, divided by the clip's length. This is exact and works for any model. It grows with both the number and the size of the frames, so it is the number that ranks providers.</li>
      <li><strong>Frames per minute.</strong> We send clips of known length, frame rate and resolution (1 second to 6 minutes, 360p to 1080p, 24 and 30 fps) and record the tokens billed for each. The model's reference preprocessing, from its published processor configuration and the vLLM or transformers video code, says how many frames each clip becomes, how they are grouped and what each group costs. Per-frame timestamp text is counted with the model's own tokenizer. A route gets a frame count only if one setting reproduces the bill for every clip to within one token: the reference itself, or the reference with a provider setting changed (frame rate, frame cap or token budget). That setting must also predict the tokens per minute billed for the reference clip.</li>
      <li>Models that merge frames before encoding them (Kimi four at a time; Qwen, GLM, Nemotron and Muse Spark two at a time) are billed per group. Frames per minute counts every frame in a group, as the model's architecture requires.</li>
      <li><strong>Not determined</strong> means the billing is consistent but no setting reproduces it exactly, so only tokens per minute is shown. <strong>Varies</strong> means the same request billed different amounts on different calls.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignored</strong> means the provider accepted the video with HTTP 200, but it added no tokens: the model never saw it.</li>
      <li>Some providers reject the 40 MB original for size or length. They get a 360p, 4-minute or 1-minute copy of the same clip, in that order, and the result says so. A provider that refuses all of them shows its numbers on the longest clip it accepted, and they don't count toward the overall score.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>OpenRouter upstreams</h2>
    <p>
      OpenRouter serves one model id from many upstream providers and picks one per request. We pin each upstream in turn
      (<code>provider.order</code> with fallbacks off) and measure it separately. When a pinned upstream can't take a
      request, OpenRouter answers "no endpoints found"; after smaller requests passed, we treat that as the upstream's limit.
    </p>

    <h2 id="overall-score">Overall score: ranking a model's providers</h2>
    <p>
      The overall score answers one question: how close does a provider come to the best provider of the same model,
      across its limits? It runs from 0 to 100 and only compares providers of one model with each other.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Limits.</strong> Six limits where higher is better: images per request, 768×432 frames, inline request
        body, images by URL, video file size and video density. Video density counts as video tokens per minute, because
        it measures both how many frames reach the model and how large they are; frames per minute alone would rank a
        provider sending tiny frames as high as one sending full-size frames. Frames per minute is shown next to it but
        not scored. Tokens per image are left out, because more tokens per image isn't better or worse in itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Each limit as a share of the best.</strong> For each limit, among the model's providers measured on it,
        a provider scores its value divided by the best provider's value, capped at 100%. A "≥" value counts at our
        probe's cap. A rejected, unsupported or ignored input scores 0%. A provider that accepts a video but ignores it (bills no tokens for it) also scores 0% on video file size, and doesn't count as the best: the file it accepted never reached the model. An inconclusive result counts at the largest
        size that passed, or is skipped if none did. A provider that refused the 5-minute video scores 0 on video
        density even if it took a shorter clip: some providers send a fixed number of frames per video, so rates per
        minute from clips of different lengths don't compare. A provider that couldn't finish the full clip in time or
        within its context window is measured on the clip's first minute, and that counts. Limits are skipped too when only one provider was measured on them
        or no provider accepts them (no DeepSeek V4.1 Flash endpoint
        takes a video file, so video file size doesn't count against any of them).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Average.</strong> The score is the plain average of those shares over the limits the provider was
        measured on, rounded. Every limit counts the same. Providers measured on fewer than two limits aren't scored.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      For example, Fireworks serves Kimi K3.1 Pro with 120 images per request, 12% of Together AI's 999, and 112 768×432
      frames, 29% of the 384 others took. Its inline body passes our cap (100%), and images by URL stop at 80 MB (63%).
      Its video file size and video density are the best measured (100% each), so its score is 67. Nebius takes more
      images and frames but no video at all, and scores 62.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read it with care. Providers aren't always measured on the same limits: most OpenRouter upstreams have only images
      and video density, while direct APIs have all six. Qwen3.8 27B has no overall score for this reason: most of its
      providers were measured on 2 limits and a few on 6. The number of limits behind each score is shown next to it.
      And because every limit weighs the same, the score is a summary; the per-limit charts show what it hides.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading a value</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A number</strong> is the largest request that passed. The first size that failed, and the provider's error text, are in the tooltip and on the model page.</li>
      <li><strong>≥ a number</strong> means nothing failed up to our probe's cap (16 MB inline, 128 MB by URL, 512 MB video, 384 frames). The real limit is higher.</li>
      <li><strong>?</strong> means the search stopped on a timeout or a transient error, so the limit is unknown.</li>
      <li><strong>No video</strong> means a <code>video_url</code> was rejected or couldn't be routed. <strong>Ignored</strong> means it was accepted but never reached the model.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Limits change.</strong> Providers update deployments without notice. Check the date, and leave headroom.</li>
      <li><strong>Limits can depend on your account.</strong> Plan, region, or a bring-your-own-key setup may change them.</li>
      <li><strong>Inline sizes depend on upload speed.</strong> Some gateways give up if the body takes too long to arrive, so very large inline requests are capped by bandwidth as much as by the provider.</li>
      <li><strong>OpenRouter results are per upstream.</strong> We pin each route to one upstream provider. Auto-routed requests can land on providers with different limits.</li>
      <li><strong>Compatibility, not quality.</strong> These numbers say what a provider accepts, not how well the model understands it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What's not here</h2>
    <p>
      This dataset only track serverless and shared endpoints. Dedicated deployments provided by the providers themselves are left out: their limits might be different.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
    <p>
      Found a limit that's wrong or out of date? <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>Open an issue</a> with the request you sent and the error you got.
    </p>
    <p>
      For anything else, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. <br />Providers: if you'd like an endpoint measured,
      tell us. <br />Teams running video analysis at scale: we're happy to talk about what Ambient
      can do for you.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
